  "heard": "95 rupees, 20 paisa. That's a litre of diesel in Delhi.  But one brokerage estimates the company selling it is losing 34 rupees on every litre.  On October 22nd, Brent crude settled above $113 after attacks in Saudi Arabia.  Jefferies says freight alone is now eight times pre-conflict levels.  In May, India raised pump prices four times in 10 days.  Diesel rose about 7 rupees 50. Five months later, it's the same 95-20.  So the gap lands on the oil companies.  How big is that gap? It depends who's counting.  ICRA said about 9 rupees in September. Jefferies now says 34.  An oil shock always gets paid for by the company, the government or you.  Right now, it's the companies. What comes next depends on crude.  Follow AVRON for the story behind the numbers."
